---
/**
 * foodcraft / LocationBM — "VISIT US" (Tahanan Cafe / BM).
 *
 * ROLE: the getting-here section. The design draws a two-part head — the
 * underlined eyebrow plus the ADDRESS set as the Libre Caslon Text headline on
 * the left, an arrival note on the right, both sitting on ONE bottom edge and
 * wrapping before either can overflow — and under it a single hairline-bordered
 * FRAME split 1.2fr / .8fr with gap:0: a drawn map face beside an opening-hours
 * column. The frame is the template's signature move: the grid is drawn with
 * BORDERS, not gaps, so the two cells share one 1px rule down the middle rather
 * than sitting apart as two cards.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every metric below is lifted verbatim from
 * "Tahanan Cafe Landing.dc.html", data-component="LOCATION" (id="visit"):
 *   head    flex · align-items:flex-end · space-between · gap 28px · wrap
 *           margin-bottom 32px; h2 margin .26em 0 0 and its OWN smaller display
 *           clamp(26px,3.3vw,44px)/1.14; sub 15px/1.78 · max-width 34ch · margin 0
 *   frame   grid minmax(0,1.2fr) minmax(0,.8fr) · GAP 0 · 1px #EDE6DF
 *           border-radius 2px · overflow hidden
 *   map     min-height 380px · ground #FAF7F3
 *           plot grid 44px at rgba(122,78,45,.08)
 *           roads  top 47% h:5px · left 44% w:4px · rgba(122,78,45,.22)
 *           pin    translate(-50%,-100%) off that crossing
 *           dot    13px #7A4E2D ringed 3px #FFFFFF
 *           flag   8px under the dot · #241B14 / #FFFFFF · 9px/.2em caps · 5px 11px
 *           caption left 18px bottom 18px · 10px/.2em caps 600 · #6B6057
 *   card    padding 30px 30px · border-left 1px #EDE6DF (the frame's spine)
 *   row     flex · space-between · gap 14px · padding 12px 0 · 1px #F2ECE5
 *           day 13px 600 #6B6057 · time Libre Caslon Text 16px #241B14, right
 *   note    13px/1.75 #6B6057 · margin 15px 0 0
 * NO LITERAL HEX APPEARS IN THIS FILE (R1). Every one of those values is written
 * as the PageBM token that already holds it: --tint --tint-2 --line --card
 * --ink --on-ink --ink-soft --muted --accent --accent-rgb --paper --disp --sans
 * --r. Nothing is re-derived to survive an admin colour scheme — that
 * re-derivation is what shipped an earlier template in this catalogue looking
 * like a different design, and PageBM's frontmatter already names --accent as
 * the one axis the theme engine is welcome to move.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts
 * under the "Location" group, so the sidebar owns a real input for each — a
 * data-field on an undeclared path is a dead hook that focuses nothing):
 *   location.tag                      — the walnut eyebrow (the wrapper's .kick)
 *   location.headline                 — the h2, AND THE ADDRESS. Rendered with
 *                                       set:html so an owner's own <em> picks up
 *                                       the design's ITALIC WALNUT EMPHASIS.
 *   location.sub                      — the arrival note, right of the head
 *   location.rules.<original i>.label — the DAY cell of an hours row
 *   location.rules.<original i>.value — the TIME cell of an hours row
 *   location.hours                    — the card's closing hours note
 *   location.lat / location.lng       — feed the Leaflet boot. No data-field:
 *                                       they are coordinates, not copy, and the
 *                                       sidebar edits them as their own inputs.
 *
 * WHY location.rules AND NOT location.hours FOR THE DAY/TIME ROWS. The schema
 * declares `location.hours` as a single kind:'text' field ("Hours line") and
 * `location.rules` as the kind:'list' of {label,value} pairs. The design's card
 * is a REPEATING two-cell row, so it needs the list. Emitting
 * `location.hours.<i>.day` instead is not a style choice, it is the exact data
 * loss the schema's own comment at that field warns about and that
 * isSchemaEditablePath was written to stop: LocationBI shipped that path, the
 * deep writer converted the existing `location.hours` STRING into an array, and
 * every other hours row vanished on the next rebuild. So the rows are
 * location.rules.*, and location.hours keeps its own scalar slot below them.
 *
 * WHERE location.hours LANDS, and why not in the card's eyebrow. The design's
 * card opens with a literal "Opening hours" label and closes with a literal
 * caveat paragraph. Both are the DESIGNER filling in their own template (R2), so
 * neither ships as written and only one of the two slots has a hook behind it.
 * location.hours takes the CLOSING PARAGRAPH, not the label, for one concrete
 * reason: an owner who types only an hours line and adds no rows then gets a
 * card that still states their hours. Put the same string in the eyebrow and
 * that owner gets a tracked-caps label with nothing underneath it — a heading
 * over nothing, which is the thing R2 exists to prevent — and the card would
 * have to be gated on rows alone, dropping their hours off the page entirely.
 *
 * THE CARD THEREFORE HAS NO EYEBROW. That is a decision, not an oversight. No
 * schema path owns a title for it, and location.tag is already spent on the
 * section eyebrow above — a second data-field on that path would be two sidebar
 * inputs fighting over one value. (Note the design's card label is NOT the .kick
 * primitive either: it is 10px at .24em with no underline, a different mark from
 * the section eyebrow, so it could not have been borrowed from the head.)
 *
 * DERIVED, printed but NOT editable here (each already owns an input in the
 * field that holds it, so a second hook on this section would be a duplicate
 * input fighting the first):
 *   layout.businessName   → the pin flag (the design's "cafeName")
 *   layout.contact.city   → the map caption (the design's "cafeTown")
 *   layout.contact.address / .lat / .lng → fallbacks behind the location.* pair
 *
 * THE HEADLINE IS THE ADDRESS, AND THERE IS NO SECOND ADDRESS PARAGRAPH. The
 * design's head is eyebrow + h2 + sub — three elements, with the h2 set to
 * `{{ addressLine }}`. There is no fourth. An earlier sibling grew an address
 * paragraph under its h2 on the reasoning that derive-content-defaults.ts fills
 * location.headline on every site while the real street line sits in
 * location.address, and it had to be taken out again: the head is a flex row
 * whose two cells share a bottom edge, so a two-line paragraph in the left cell
 * drags the sub down a row and strands it on the right beside nothing. That
 * paragraph is not built here. The address is not lost by leaving it out — it
 * feeds the geocode below (tier 2 of the map, which is how this section finds
 * its own pin) and FooterBM prints it, so location.address keeps a live sidebar
 * input either way.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED:
 *   · location.phone. The design's LOCATION prints no number; the closing band
 *     and the footer own it. A tel: row here would be a control this design does
 *     not have (R2).
 *   · location.directions.text/href. Schema-declared and used by siblings, but
 *     this design's LOCATION has no directions button. Adding one would be
 *     building a different section than the one that was handed over.
 *   · NO .plate / .ph / role="img" (R9). The map cell holds no upload — it is a
 *     DRAWING of a map — so it gets neither the wrapper's image frame nor an
 *     image role. The only accessible name available for it would be a sentence
 *     I wrote ("Map showing …"), which is exactly the component-supplied copy R2
 *     and R9 forbid. The whole drawn face, roads, pin and flag included, is
 *     therefore aria-hidden ONCE, on the face itself and nowhere else — a
 *     literal aria-hidden beside a conditional one is the duplicated-attribute
 *     bug two earlier components shipped — and everything it pictures is
 *     announced as real text beside it: the address headline, the caption and
 *     the hours rows.
 *   · NO STAR ROW (R2). This section carries no rating field and no review. Five
 *     stars printed for a customer who gave no rating is a fabricated claim;
 *     stars belong to TESTIMONIALS and only ever from a numeric rating.
 *   · NO onPage() ANCHOR-REMAP HELPER (R7), and this too is a decision. The
 *     helper exists to rewrite in-page hrefs onto this page's ids, and this
 *     section emits NO href at all — the map is not a link, the card is not a
 *     link, and the design draws no button here, so a copied helper would ship
 *     as dead code. The SECTION ID is the part of R7 that does apply and it is
 *     honoured: id="visit", exactly as the design writes it and exactly the
 *     anchor PageBM's nav targets. If a link is ever added here, copy the helper
 *     from a BM sibling with PAGE_ANCHORS = menu · about · gallery · visit · faq
 *     · main and ANCHOR_ALIAS mapping location,contact → visit · services,rooms
 *     → menu · host,why → about · work → gallery · book → main, passing tel: /
 *     mailto: / http through untouched.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5). Body content only: the map (which needs real coordinates OR
 * a real address behind it), the hours rows, the hours note, or the arrival
 * paragraph. NOT the tag and NOT the headline — derive-content-defaults.ts emits
 * location.tag and location.headline for EVERY site, so a gate that counted them
 * would be always-true and would ship an eyebrow and a heading over an empty
 * bordered hole on every first build. The frame is gated again on its own, so a
 * site with only a sub keeps its paragraph and grows no empty box under it, and
 * either cell can leave on its own with the survivor taking the full width — and
 * losing the spine, because a hairline down the left of a full-width card is a
 * grid line for a column that is not there.
 *
 * THE MAP, three tiers, the pattern the family shares:
 *   1. build-time lat/lng → window.__foodcraftBMInitMap(lat, lng, label)
 *   2. else any address   → client-side Nominatim lookup, then the same boot
 *   3. else               → the drawn plot-grid face, which IS the design
 * The drawn face lives INSIDE #map until Leaflet mounts; PageBM's boot runs
 * el.innerHTML = '' first, and that is what erases it. The literal id="map" and
 * the exact global name __foodcraftBMInitMap are a contract with PageBM — rename
 * either and the drawn face silently stays on screen underneath the tiles. The
 * name is family-unique because a preview harness can render two wrappers in one
 * document and a shared name cross-wires the maps.
 *
 * A CLEARED coordinate field leaves '', which passes != null and which Number('')
 * turns into 0 — booting Leaflet and pinning the café at 0,0 in the Gulf of
 * Guinea with its own name on the marker: a map asserting a location the owner
 * never gave. num() rejects blank and out-of-range values so a cleared field
 * falls through to the address lookup instead, and it uses ?? rather than || so
 * a genuine 0 survives.
 *
 * THE HEADLINE IS THE ONE PLACE THIS FILE RESTATES A FONT-SIZE, and it is the
 * design's own: LOCATION is set at clamp(26px,3.3vw,44px) where the wrapper's
 * shared .h2 is clamp(29px,3.8vw,50px). Four of the design's fourteen headlines
 * step down like this and LOCATION is one of them, so the primitive is not the
 * spec here. R8's clamp trap was checked at every boundary rather than assumed:
 * the restated clamp is declared ONCE, at base, and is touched by no media query
 * in this file, so it evaluates 44px ≥ 1334px, 38.3px at 1160, 29.7px at 900 and
 * its 26px floor at every width below ~788px — monotonic, never growing as the
 * viewport narrows. Confining it to a min-width query instead, to let the
 * wrapper's 760 pass own mobile, is what WOULD have produced the inversion: the
 * shared pass is clamp(26px,7vw,38px), so the headline would have JUMPED from
 * 26px to 38px the moment the viewport crossed from 761px to 760px. A scoped
 * class out-specifies the wrapper's :where(.h2), so this rule knowingly takes
 * the 760 pass's font-size off this one heading; the same pass's `max-width:
 * none` and `overflow-wrap: anywhere` are NOT restated and still land.
 *
 * TWO OTHER VALUES RESTATED OFF A WRAPPER PRIMITIVE. Neither is a new choice:
 * each puts back a number the design states and the primitive rounds off.
 *   · The arrival note is a FLAT 15px at 1.78. .body is clamp(14.5px,1.3vw,15px)
 *     at 1.8, which reaches the design's size only above a ~1150px viewport and
 *     prints 14.5px on every laptop below it.
 *   · The row cells, the flag and the caption take `line-height: normal`. The
 *     design sets no leading on any of them, so `normal` is literally what it
 *     draws, where PageBM's body leading of 1.78 leaks in and inflates an hours
 *     row from the drawn ~43px to ~52px. `normal` rather than a number, because
 *     WhyBM already made this call for the same reason: guessing a number would
 *     be a divergence of its own.
 *
 * TWO FORCED DEVIATIONS, both small, both for the same reason — the design draws
 * this cell as a static picture and ours can turn into a live tile map:
 *   1. The pin flag DROPS white-space: nowrap. The frame clips overflow, so a
 *      long business name would be sliced off at both ends; wrapping beats
 *      truncating on the element that names the café. The stack is centred on
 *      the 44% crossing, so it is capped at twice the smaller side — 88% — less
 *      12px of air each side, and width: max-content is required alongside that
 *      or absolute shrink-to-fit collapses it to (containing block − left).
 *   2. The caption gains an OPAQUE --tint backing. In the design it sits on the
 *      drawn tint face; the moment Leaflet paints CARTO tiles underneath,
 *      unbacked 10px tracked caps are unreadable over street labels. The backing
 *      keeps the design's exact colour pair (--muted on --tint) rather than
 *      swapping in the wrapper's ink .cap pill, which would have changed the
 *      design's palette to solve a legibility problem.
 * A THIRD, smaller: the flag and the caption backing take border-radius var(--r)
 * where the design gives those two surfaces no radius at all. 2px is this
 * template's universal corner — the frame, the buttons and the .cap primitive
 * all carry it — and 0 would leave the only square corners on the page sitting
 * on the two smallest surfaces in it. The difference is a fraction of a pixel of
 * shape; the inconsistency would not be.
 *
 * COLOUR PAIRING ON THE FLAG. The design's flag is #241B14 on #FFFFFF, which is
 * --ink on white. It is written --ink / --on-ink rather than --ink / --on-band:
 * both are #FFFFFF natively so the design renders identically either way, but
 * buildOverrideCss recomputes --on-ink against a scheme's ink while --on-band is
 * pinned to the closing band's fixed espresso, so only the first pair stays
 * legible if an admin scheme moves --ink.
 *
 * VERIFIED CONTRAST, this section's own pairs (sRGB, the design's palette):
 *   ink-soft/paper 7.71 (the arrival note) · accent/paper 7.11 (the eyebrow)
 *   ink/paper 16.91 (the headline, the time cell) · on-ink/ink 16.91 (the flag)
 *   muted/card 6.11 (the day cell, the closing note)
 *   muted/tint 5.72 (the map caption, on its backing)
 * Nothing in this section is below AA — unlike the previous template in this
 * family, whose day cell and caption shipped at ~3.6:1, this design's own
 * small-print colour already clears the bar and nothing had to be recoloured.
 *
 * SECTION PADDING is the wrapper's, not this file's. The design writes
 * `max-width:1160px;margin:0 auto;padding:84px 30px 0`: the 1160/gutter half is
 * the shared .wrap, and the 84px top is html[data-page="BM"] section's
 * clamp(52px,7vw,84px), which caps at exactly that.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;
const lay = layout || {};

const loc = content.location || {};
const tag = typeof loc.tag === 'string' ? loc.tag.trim() : '';
// Nothing beyond trim(): the headline goes out through set:html so whatever
// markup the owner typed reaches the wrapper's italic-walnut <em> rule.
const headline = typeof loc.headline === 'string' ? loc.headline.trim() : '';
const sub = typeof loc.sub === 'string' ? loc.sub.trim() : '';
// The card's closing note. The schema's single "Hours line" text field — see the
// frontmatter for why it lands here and not in the card's eyebrow.
const hoursNote = typeof loc.hours === 'string' ? loc.hours.trim() : '';

// READ, NOT PRINTED. The design's head is eyebrow + h2 + sub and its h2 IS the
// address; there is no second address paragraph anywhere in that section. This
// value feeds the geocode below (tier 2 of the map) and FooterBM prints it.
const address = String(loc.address ?? lay.contact?.address ?? '').trim();

// A row the owner added but never filled would spend a hairline and 24px of
// padding saying nothing. Drop the blanks (whitespace counts as blank) but carry
// the ORIGINAL index so deleting row 2 cannot misdirect an edit into row 3 (R6),
// and note that this filter is what makes the gate honest: a rules array of
// nothing but blanks no longer keeps the card alive.
type HourRow = { i: number; day: string; time: string };
const rows: HourRow[] = (Array.isArray(loc.rules) ? loc.rules : [])
  .map((r: any, i: number) => ({
    i,
    day: String(r?.label ?? '').trim(),
    time: String(r?.value ?? '').trim(),
  }))
  .filter((r: HourRow) => Boolean(r.day || r.time));

const num = (v: any, max: number): number | null => {
  if (v === null || v === undefined || String(v).trim() === '') return null;
  const n = Number(v);
  return Number.isFinite(n) && Math.abs(n) <= max ? n : null;
};
const lat = num(loc.lat, 90) ?? num(lay.contact?.lat, 90);
const lng = num(loc.lng, 180) ?? num(lay.contact?.lng, 180);
const hasCoords = lat !== null && lng !== null;
const hasAddress = !!address;
// The drawn face is a picture claiming where this café physically is. With
// neither coordinates nor an address there is nothing real behind that claim, so
// the whole cell leaves and the card takes the full width.
const hasMap = hasCoords || hasAddress;

// The pin flag and the marker popup. No placeholder: with no business name the
// flag leaves and the pin is a bare dot, rather than a label reading "Your
// Business" over a real street.
const brand = String(lay.businessName ?? '').trim();
// The design's "cafeTown". Nothing derives it, so on a site without a city it is
// simply absent rather than guessed out of the address.
const locality = String(lay.contact?.city ?? '').trim();

const hasCard = rows.length > 0 || !!hoursNote;
const hasFrame = hasMap || hasCard;
const hasVisit = hasFrame || !!sub;
---
{hasVisit && (
<section class="tah-visit" id="visit">
  <div class="wrap">
    {/* TWO children, exactly as the design draws it: the heading block and the
        arrival note beside it. No address paragraph — see the frontmatter. */}
    <div class="tah-visit-head reveal">
      <div class="tah-visit-head-l">
        {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="location.tag">{tag}</span>}
        {headline && <h2 class="h2 tah-visit-h2" data-field="location.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
      </div>
      {sub && <p class="body tah-visit-sub" data-field="location.sub">{sub}</p>}
    </div>
    {hasFrame && (
      /* ONE bordered frame, gap:0 — the two cells share the spine rather than
         standing apart. `is-one` collapses it to a single track and takes the
         spine off when only one cell survives the guards. */
      <div class:list={['tah-visit-frame', 'reveal', (!hasMap || !hasCard) && 'is-one']} data-delay="1">
        {hasMap && (
          <div class="tah-visit-map">
            <div id="map">
              {/* Tier-3 only, and entirely decorative: PageBM's boot empties #map
                  before Leaflet draws, so tiles never stack on top of this. One
                  aria-hidden, here and nowhere else in the subtree. */}
              <div class="tah-visit-face" aria-hidden="true">
                <span class="tah-visit-road tah-visit-road-h"></span>
                <span class="tah-visit-road tah-visit-road-v"></span>
                <span class="tah-visit-pin">
                  <span class="tah-visit-dot"></span>
                  {brand && <span class="tah-visit-flag">{brand}</span>}
                </span>
              </div>
            </div>
            {/* OUTSIDE #map on purpose: the city is the owner's real text, not
                fallback chrome, so it has to survive the boot that erases the
                face — and it stays in the accessibility tree. */}
            {locality && <span class="mono tah-visit-cap">{locality}</span>}
          </div>
        )}
        {hasCard && (
          <div class="tah-visit-card">
            {rows.map((r) => (
              /* Both cells always render, even when one is blank: the row is
                 space-between, so dropping an empty cell would promote its
                 sibling to the wrong edge. An empty span draws nothing — the
                 guard that matters ran above, where a wholly blank row was
                 dropped along with its hairline. */
              <div class="rowrule tah-visit-row">
                <span class="tah-visit-d" data-field={`location.rules.${r.i}.label`}>{r.day}</span>
                <span class="tah-visit-t" data-field={`location.rules.${r.i}.value`}>{r.time}</span>
              </div>
            ))}
            {hoursNote && <p class="tah-visit-note" data-field="location.hours">{hoursNote}</p>}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>
    )}
  </div>
</section>
)}
{hasCoords && (
  <script is:inline define:vars={{ lat, lng, brand }}>
    (function () {
      // Bounded like the geocode branch below: this script is emitted where the
      // section sits, ABOVE the wrapper's boot, so one retry is the normal case —
      // but an unbounded loop would spin a timer forever on any page where the
      // wrapper script never ran at all.
      var attempt = 0;
      (function init() {
        if (window.__foodcraftBMInitMap) { window.__foodcraftBMInitMap(lat, lng, brand); }
        else if (attempt++ < 25) { setTimeout(init, 80); }
      })();
    })();
  </script>
)}
{!hasCoords && hasAddress && (
  <script is:inline define:vars={{ address, brand }}>
    (function geocodeThenInit() {
      var attempt = 0;
      function tryInit(la, ln) {
        if (window.__foodcraftBMInitMap) { window.__foodcraftBMInitMap(la, ln, brand); }
        else if (attempt++ < 25) { setTimeout(function () { tryInit(la, ln); }, 80); }
      }
      try {
        var query = encodeURIComponent(String(address).replace(/\n/g, ', '));
        var url = 'https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=json&limit=1&q=' + query;
        fetch(url, { headers: { 'Accept-Language': 'en' } })
          .then(function (r) { return r.ok ? r.json() : []; })
          .then(function (results) {
            if (!Array.isArray(results) || results.length === 0) return;
            var la = parseFloat(results[0].lat);
            var ln = parseFloat(results[0].lon);
            if (isNaN(la) || isNaN(ln)) return;
            tryInit(la, ln);
          })
          .catch(function () { /* keep the drawn face: it is the design */ });
      } catch (e) { /* never block render */ }
    })();
  </script>
)}
<style>
  /* ── THE HEAD · flex, bottom-aligned, space-between, wrapping before either
        side can overflow. 32px to the frame, straight from the design.
        TWO children only. A third — an address paragraph under the h2 — is what
        dragged the sub down a row in an earlier sibling, because in this row a
        taller left cell moves everything aligned to its bottom edge. */
  .tah-visit-head {
    display: flex; align-items: flex-end; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 28px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin: 0 0 32px;
  }
  /* min-width:0 so a long unbroken address cannot widen the cell past the row
     and push the page sideways (R8). */
  .tah-visit-head-l { min-width: 0; }
  /* The eyebrow is NOT restated here — only wrapped. Restating .kick's font or
     colour would drop the border-bottom that IS this design's signature, and
     restating its font-size would silently cancel the wrapper's 520 pass. */
  .tah-visit-head .kick { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  /* The design's OWN display step for this section: clamp(26px,3.3vw,44px),
     smaller than the shared .h2's clamp(29px,3.8vw,50px). Declared once, at
     base, and touched by no media query in this file — see the frontmatter for
     the boundary-by-boundary check and for why a min-width query would have
     re-created the inversion an audit found on the previous template.
     line-height 1.14 and --ink come from the primitive; the wrapper's 24ch
     measure is kept deliberately, because the design sets no max-width here but
     the head is a two-cell flex row and an uncapped address line would eat the
     cell the arrival note sits in. 24ch is generous for a street line. */
  .tah-visit-h2 {
    margin: .26em 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(26px, 3.3vw, 44px);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* set:html output carries no scope attribute, so an owner's <em> inside the
     headline is only reachable through :global() (R3). PageBM already paints
     .h2 em and .h2 .em italic walnut globally; this pair restates the wrapper's
     own values so the signature survives even if that global rule is ever
     narrowed, and invents nothing. */
  .tah-visit-h2 :global(em),
  .tah-visit-h2 :global(.em) { font-style: italic; color: var(--accent); }
  /* Qualified with the parent class on purpose: the wrapper's bare-paragraph
     reset is (0,1,1) and it sets a bottom margin this must not inherit.
     .body supplies the design's --ink-soft, but not its size or its leading:
     .body is clamp(14.5px,1.3vw,15px) at 1.8, where the design states a flat
     15px at 1.78 and prints it at every width. */
  .tah-visit-head .tah-visit-sub {
    margin: 0; min-width: 0; max-width: 34ch;
    font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.78;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── THE FRAME · the design's 1.2fr / .8fr, GAP 0, one hairline around the
        pair and one down the middle. This is the template's grid-drawn-with-
        borders signature, so the gutter is not "tightened", it is absent.
        Either cell can be absent too — no coordinates and no address drops the
        map, no rows and no note drops the card — and then `is-one` gives the
        survivor the full width AND removes the spine, because a hairline down
        the left of a full-width card is a grid line for a column that is not
        there. */
  .tah-visit-frame {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.2fr) minmax(0, .8fr);
    gap: 0; align-items: stretch;
    border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--r);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .tah-visit-frame.is-one { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }

  /* ── THE MAP CELL. Not the wrapper's .plate: that frame is for an upload and
        carries its own --well ground, a radius and a min-height, where the
